I thought the original intention was to make bones files portable
across platforms, not across versions. This would be a Good Thing (tm).


Portability across versions is very unlikely. Sure, there are hacks for
big things like adding a new object, but there are also small changes
which just affect the meaning of certain fields on existing objects.

For example, in a patch I'm working on, I'm reinterpreting the value of
obj->spe on chest objects (to indicate the difficulty of the lock). Old
save/bones files will still load, but will not make sense in the new
version. This can't really be solved without adding a lot of special
load/save code for translating one version into another. And I don't
think anyone is really interested in that.

It seems to me that a large part of the code for loading/saving could
(and should) be generated automatically. One of the advantages of the
current arrangement is that if I add a new field to the 'obj' struct, I
don't have to worry about writing code to load/save it, because it just
gets written as part of the struct. It would be good if this could
continue.

Does anyone know of a utility which takes as input the source file
defining I C struct and outputs appropriate C source for loading/saving
it in XML? I don't imagine it should be too difficult. It would be a
very handy thing to have.

